WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.071
Scenario: A sticker covers the QR code on a parking meter.

2
00:00:04.071 --> 00:00:07.403
The replacement code opens a page asking for card

3
00:00:07.403 --> 00:00:10.734
details and vehicle information. Safest response: Option A: Use

4
00:00:10.734 --> 00:00:14.065
the official payment app or website reached independently and

5
00:00:14.065 --> 00:00:17.396
inspect/report the altered code. QR codes conceal URLs; use

6
00:00:17.396 --> 00:00:20.727
a known official payment route and report suspected tampering.

7
00:00:20.727 --> 00:00:24.058
Option B is unsafe because Sharing can spread the

8
00:00:24.058 --> 00:00:27.390
malicious destination and does not verify its operator. Option

9
00:00:27.390 --> 00:00:30.721
C is unsafe because A sticker can be placed

10
00:00:30.721 --> 00:00:34.792
over a legitimate QR code; physical placement does not prove authenticity.

11
00:00:34.792 --> 00:00:38.123
Option D is unsafe because Logos can be copied

12
00:00:38.123 --> 00:00:41.455
and do not prove the destination is legitimate. In

13
00:00:41.455 --> 00:00:44.786
practice: Do not enter details on the linked page.

14
00:00:44.786 --> 00:00:48.117
Use the operator’s official app or a known address

15
00:00:48.117 --> 00:00:51.448
and report the altered sticker. Key takeaway: A QR

16
00:00:51.448 --> 00:00:54.779
code is simply a link in a different format;

17
00:00:54.779 --> 00:00:57.000
inspect and verify it before acting.
